# PART 6 — REMOVE HARDCODED CREATIONS AND PROJECT LINKS

## 8. Make the public showcase fully data-driven

This is a very important requirement.

The current website contains sample projects, games, website previews and other example content.

I do not want hardcoded games, hardcoded website URLs, sample repositories or fictitious project links appearing as actual public content.

Inspect the existing project showcase, mock data and all components responsible for displaying creations.

Remove the hardcoded demonstration content from the live production experience.

Do not leave fake games or arbitrary external links in the All Creations section.

The public showcase must load real content from the existing Firestore database.

### Approved public content sources

Public creations may come from two places:

**Source 1: Administrator-published content**

An administrator can create a public creation or project directly from the administration interface.

**Source 2: Private Community Hub submissions**

Students may share games, websites, videos, novels, experiments and other creations inside the existing private Community Hub.

Those submissions must initially remain private to the channels and users who are authorised to access them.

An administrator may subsequently select one or more of those shared creations for public showcasing.

Students should also be able to request that their creations be considered for public showcasing.

No private chat link should automatically become publicly accessible simply because it has been shared in a channel.

### Important publication rule

Only records explicitly approved and published for public viewing should appear on the main website.

The public website must not fetch and render private chat messages directly as public content.

Create or update an appropriately controlled public publication record when content has been approved.

Preserve the original source message and its private-channel permissions.

If a student submits a creation for public consideration, the request must be reviewed before publication.

