# PART 8 — ALL SHARED LINKS AND UNREAD ACTIVITY

## 10. Fix the All Shared Links content-loading problem

There is currently a problem with All Shared Links.

Some shared links do not appear immediately. It sometimes seems that more than one item is missing until the user opens the section.

This must be investigated and corrected.

Do not simply change the displayed item count.

Inspect the actual Firestore queries, subscriptions, pagination, caching and rendering logic.

### Expected behaviour

All Shared Links should contain the complete collection of shared links that the authenticated user is authorised to access across the relevant Community Hub channels.

Every valid shared-link message should be represented.

The section should:

- Load its available history reliably.
- Display new links as they arrive.
- Update automatically through the existing real-time infrastructure.
- Preserve chronological ordering.
- Avoid duplicated records.
- Avoid dropping messages because of incorrect query limits.
- Handle pagination correctly.
- Display older links when the user requests them.
- Retain the original channel information and sender details.
- Respect existing private-channel access permissions.

The section must not depend on the user opening it for the first time before the content becomes visible or loads correctly.

Investigate the current implementation and fix the actual issue.

### Unread/new activity indicators

Improve the visibility of new shared links and activity.

When new messages or links arrive in All Shared Links or a relevant channel, display a clear unread/new-activity indicator.

Use the existing brand palette:

- Yellow: #FFF025.
- Pink: #B11469.
- Orange: #C15D00.

A compact yellow or pink unread badge is preferable.

The badge may display a meaningful unread count or a small indicator when a numerical count is unavailable.

Do not add unnecessary green badges or unrelated status colours.

### Unread count behaviour

- Count genuinely unread incoming messages or links.
- Do not count the current user's own messages as incoming unread messages where inappropriate.
- Update the count in real time.
- Clear the unread state when the relevant section is opened or messages are marked as read.
- Preserve the read state across refreshes and authenticated sessions.
- Avoid counting the same message more than once.
- Do not display fake unread counts.

If the existing interface already has a numerical count, preserve it and correct its underlying logic.

Apply consistent unread indicators to other relevant channels where new activity occurs.

