# PART 12 — DATA AND TECHNICAL IMPLEMENTATION

## 14. Make the content system genuinely dynamic

Inspect existing Firestore collections before adding new ones.

Reuse existing data structures where practical.

Avoid duplicate collections, redundant records or parallel content-management systems.

The system should be able to distinguish between:

- Private chat messages.
- Private creation submissions.
- Public showcase requests.
- Publicly approved creations.
- Public discussion posts.
- Official announcements.
- Challenges.
- Public categories.
- User notifications.

Use clear publication states and ownership metadata.

All public data must be read from approved Firestore records.

Do not use `SAMPLE_PROJECTS`, mock discussions or hardcoded project URLs as production public content.

Demo data may remain in an explicitly isolated development/demo environment if required for testing, but must never masquerade as real public community activity.

Do not expose private Firestore data through public collection queries.

### Firestore security

Review the actual existing security rules.

Ensure:

- Public visitors can read only published public content.
- Authenticated public users can perform permitted public interactions.
- Only authorised users can create submissions.
- Private chat messages remain restricted.
- Only approved users can access protected Community Hub data.
- Only authorised administrators can approve and publish curated content.
- Users cannot make their own submissions publicly approved by modifying their own Firestore fields.
- Users cannot modify their own administrator roles or membership status.
- Private student application information remains protected.

Any change in publication status that exposes content publicly must be performed through a trusted, authorised administrative process.

